changesets.body:
- a type scale with no base or ratio, or one that is no block, which builds every size as NaNrem;
- a role or a typography given as a plain value, which builds nothing;
- a heading `weights` that is no block of levels, which Core reads letter by letter;
- one heading level written twice (`'01'` and `1`), where Core keeps one.

Without a type scale, any role weight gets the no-scale reason, and an empty weight is named as skipped. A boxed value is judged as its primitive, a signed number weight (`-100`) is read as written, and a plain-object theme is judged on the values it loads.

- a bound already passed the way the words ask ("input borders thinner but at least 2px" at 1px, "the corners rounder but at most 4px", "borders thicker but no more than 0px", "the text bigger but no bigger than 12px", "the line height bigger but at most 1.2", "card padding smaller but no less than 64px") changes nothing and says so, never moving the value the other way; a part's border width is read from the token its border reads, and a way beside a bound on a length the part does not have ("cards wider but at most 300px") writes nothing;
- "headings tighter" is the headings dial's letter spacing, one rung from where it is, and "but no tighter than -0.01em" holds it there instead of changing the whole theme's spacing;
- "buttons 4px taller" reads the part's height, and "8px wider" its inline padding, half on each side;
- "selected rows lighter" steps the token Core paints a selected row with, in the mode named;
- "the header the same color as the sidebar" takes the page color a part with no fill of its own shows;
- "more colorful" and "less saturated" on a one-mode theme answer with the first vibrancy that moves the accents it shows the way asked;
- a value question on a one-mode theme tells the mode it shows, and says a mode it never shows has no value;
- "the accent color but lighter", "the danger color but darker" (with or without a comma before "but") make that color lighter or darker in each mode on a fill or a border, as on text;
- "the sidebar the page color but darker", "the header the card color but darker": the color of the part named after another is read and stepped, never changed itself.

- moves only the control height of the size named when one is ("make the small buttons 4px taller"), on the part;
- reads a possessive owner's own element ("the dropdown menu's items") as that element, never the owner;
- treats a later "the shadows softer" or "the corners rounder" as a change to the theme's shadows or corners, never to the part named before.

- reads "twice as soft", "twice as faint" and "twice as subtle" as half of now, so shadows get softer, not stronger;
- reads an owner named with a possessive ("the menus' separators") as where the change goes, the same as "separators in menus", and never paints the owner;
- sets a divider's thickness on the variant asked for ("the strong divider thicker"), not on every divider.

- "make input borders 3:1 contrast" gives the part's border color the least change that measures that ratio against the page and the part's own fill (or the part after "against"), in each mode: its own ink at a higher strength, else a deeper shade of it. It no longer restates the border the part already draws;
- "make the card text 50% more transparent" scales the text colors on the card from their own alpha, instead of fading the whole card; a part's border or fill, each variant on its own, and "all shadows" take the same relative change;
- "what is the corner radius", "what is the spacing", "what font is used", "what is the border width", "what color is the danger" answer with the theme's value in each mode it shows, and "what is the heading font" answers with the heading's font;
- "make links the color of the text", "make badges the color of the danger" take that theme color;
- "make card shadows lighter" says the card draws no shadow of its own at rest, instead of a false reason;
- "a gradient from #ff0000 to transparent" keeps the typed transparent stop instead of ending in a lighter shade;
- "what is the hover background of ghost buttons" reads the state it names: the fill at rest, with the hover or press wash Core draws over it;
- "borders 30% more transparent", "the shadow 20% more opaque", "the accent 50% more transparent" change those colors' own alpha (never the border width);
- "body font weight 450" writes 450; "how dense is it" and "what is the density" answer with the spacing instead of changing it; "shadows sharper" halves the shared shadows' blur instead of softening them; "the borders on cards heavier" makes the borders wider with no false line.

- when a build of `<theme>.theme.ts` reads another export named for the theme (a default export beside `<theme>Theme`), the refusal says the build reads that export, not the one that extends the settings, instead of asking to put back what is already there;
- a value Core refuses inside a rule it derives from the typography is no longer reported as a value of the file; Core's refusal of a local token's name is named at its line;
- an editor theme that lost `<theme>.settings.json` is refused by `compile` and `compile --check` as the editor commands refuse it, with the line that makes it again;
- a refused value in a part block imported from a file beside the theme (`components: parts`, `parts.button`), or in a theme the source re-exports (`export * from './impl'`), names that file and line.

- "make cards the color of the page" gives cards the page's color and never changes the page ("but 10% darker" steps that color in each mode; an amount before "lighter" or "darker" after "but" stays with the ask);
- "make dialog shadows 50% more transparent", "20% less opaque" scale each shadow layer's own alpha, in each mode, instead of writing one alpha on every layer; a part that draws no shadow of its own says so;
- "make borders 3:1 contrast" gives the border color at the lowest strength that measures that ratio against the page, surfaces and cards in each mode, instead of a fainter border;
- a bound on a whole-theme ask holds: "padding bigger but at most 2px" (the base spacing step is past it) and "corners square but at least 8px" (square is 0px) write nothing and say why;
- "make shadows use 20% black" sets each shared shadow's layers in that color, offsets kept, and a color written as a function (color-mix(…, transparent)) is never read as a "transparent" ask;
- "what color is the text", "what color is the accent", "what is the body text size" answer with the theme's value in each mode it shows;
- "more colorful" and "less saturated" are judged by how colorful the accents look (OKLCH chroma, the measure vibrancy moves), so a pale accent such as butter's dark yellow can be made more colorful.

- a part's own state in other letters or with a space, an underscore or a colon (toggle-button's `IsPressed`, `is pressed`, `is_pressed`, `:isPressed` for isPressed);
- a CSS property in other letters (`Background-Color` for background-color).

A state's prop name typed or saved in one case (`--state ispressed` or `ISPRESSED` on button, `isselected` on tab) is that state, as `isPressed` and `aria-pressed` already were.

- a `--when status:<x>` setting now wins over the part's plain one-mode color whichever was set first: the variant blocks a plain color is written into come before every other condition, so an accepted status border always shows;
- `how` value questions read what the part draws: "what is the focus ring of inputs in dark mode" gives the ring it shows when focused (no longer the mode dial), "what color is the toast text" gives the color on the toast's own inverted surface, and "what is the shadow of cards" gives none at rest instead of the selected card's ring;
- "make all borders red in dark mode" gives every step: a line a part does not draw yet gets its color see-through in the other mode, so the whole answer runs.

- a field `defineTheme()` or an `onDark`/`onLight` block does not take (`component`, `tokenz`), with the field meant, read from the installed Core's own types;
- a `tokens` key one typo from a design token (`--color-acent`), pointing a property of the theme's own to `localTokens`;
- a part's pseudo-class one typo from one browsers know (`:hovr`), a part variable one typo from one the part reads that nothing in the theme reads (`--button-focus-ofset`), and a part variable's value of the wrong kind for its default;
- an unknown component (`cardd`, or a part named by its class), and a component entry that holds no block of properties;
- a typography role or field Core does not read, a heading level outside 1–6, a scale with no ratio, and the `fallbacks` field when it is what a family refuses;
- a part's `fontFamily` read as `set` reads a family list, and a `var()` of the theme's own property read as the text it holds;
- a `var()` of the wrong kind inside a token's function (`color-mix(in srgb, var(--radius-element) 50%, red)`), in the source and in `set`;
- a value in a theme the export extends from another file, named in that file;
- a path Core itself refuses (`adaptations.rules[0].when.pointr`), at its line. `theme build` gives the import hint only when an import did not resolve.

Before:

```tsx
<ListItem
  label="Inbox"
  swipeActions={{trailing: [{label: 'Archive', onActivate: archive}]}}
  swipeBehavior="commit"
/>
```

After:

```tsx
import {createSwipe} from '@astryxdesign/core/Swipe';

const inboxSwipe = createSwipe({
  trailing: [
    {
      id: 'archive',
      label: 'Archive',
      onActivate: ({itemKey}) => archive(itemKey),
    },
  ],
  swipeBehavior: 'commit',
});

<ListItem label="Inbox" itemKey={message.id} swipe={inboxSwipe} />;
```
